---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/hero-home.avif";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";

// Hero, card and both portraits are generated by `pnpm artwork` from
// hand-authored SVG (tools/make-artwork.mjs). 404.md points at this same
// hero path.
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="A bar interior reduced to flat gold and grey shapes — a mullioned window, a back shelf of bottles, a counter, and three empty stools — in a two-ink risograph print"
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <p class="lede">
    There is a pub near campus where, at half past four on a Tuesday, the same
    four people are at the same end of the bar. Nobody arranged it. Two of them
    do not know each other's surnames. This course is about that room, and the
    barbershop, the laundromat, the platform and the guild hall.
  </p>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    You will watch one place all semester. Every Thursday is a <strong>Round</strong>
    — a field visit with a brief — and you will sit in pubs, barbershops,
    laundromats, waiting rooms, archives and a game, counting who is there and
    writing it down on the premises. Monday lectures give you the theory to
    argue with: belonging as a drive, exclusion measured in a lab, weak ties,
    social capital and the mortality data.
  </p>
  <p>
    You walk out with three things: four sets of raw field notes and an honest
    page on what they were blind to, a 2500-word audit of one real place, and a
    proposal that a particular place — with an address — should exist, defended
    in front of a room that has spent twelve weeks learning what to ask.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    Third-year students in psychology, sociology, human geography, design or
    anything adjacent. You need no statistics beyond reading an effect size and
    being sceptical of it, and no fieldwork experience — the method is taught
    from the first week.
  </p>
  <p>
    You do need to be willing to sit alone in a public room for an hour without
    your phone, to be a paying customer in somebody's business, and to write
    down what was actually there rather than what you expected. Students who
    want a lecture course with a reading list should look elsewhere; two thirds
    of the mark rests on places you physically went.
  </p>

  <h2>A warning about the register</h2>
  <p>
    This is not a celebration of third places. By week 3 the course is arguing
    that Oldenburg's eight characteristics never say who a room keeps out, and
    by week 4 that the warmth inside and the sharpness of the edge are the same
    mechanism. If you arrive fond of these places, you should leave more
    precise about them and slightly less fond.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>Twelve field visits, each with a brief and a method. Start here.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Lectures" href="/lectures/">
      <p>What is argued on Mondays, week by week.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Readings" href="/readings/">
      <p>One paper a week, what it claims, and why this course sets it.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>Field Notes, the Place Audit, and the Proposal. 25 / 30 / 45.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>An ethnographer of laundromats and a former publican.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Policies" href="/policies/">
      <p>Where you may observe, where you may not, and the rules in the field.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .lede {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--at-border, currentColor);
    padding-left: 1rem;
    margin-block-end: 2.5rem;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
</style>
